===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|resonance}} (French {{m|fr|résonance}}), from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|resonantia||echo}}, from {{m|la|resonō||I resound}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɹɛzənəns/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|resonances}}

# The condition of being [[resonant]].
#* {{quote-news
|date=May 24, 2012
|author=Nathan Rabin
|title=Film: Reviews: Men In Black 3
|work=The Onion AV Club
|url=http://www.avclub.com/articles/men-in-black-3,75576/
|page=
|passage=But the film is largely redeemed by an unexpected emotional '''resonance''' befitting a Steven Spielberg production.}}
# A resonant sound, [[echo]], or [[reverberation]], such as that produced by blowing over the top of a bottle.
# {{label|en|medicine}} The sound produced by a [[hollow]] body part such as the chest cavity upon [[auscultation]], especially that produced while the patient is speaking. 
# {{label|en|figuratively}} Something that [[evoke]]s an [[association]], or a strong [[emotion]].
# {{label|en|physics}} The [[increase]] in the [[amplitude]] of an [[oscillation]] of a [[system]] under the influence of a [[periodic]] [[force]] whose [[frequency]] is close to that of the system's [[natural]] frequency.
# {{label|en|nuclear physics}} A short-lived [[subatomic particle]] or state of [[atomic]] [[excitation]] that results from the [[collision]] of atomic [[particle]]s.
#* '''2004''', When experiments with the first ‘atom-smashers’ took place in the 1950s to 1960s, many short-lived heavier siblings of the proton and neutron, known as ‘'''resonances'''’, were discovered. — Frank Close, ''Particle Physics: A Very Short Introduction'' (Oxford 2004, p. 35)
# An increase in the [[strength]] or [[duration]] of a [[musical]] [[tone]] produced by [[sympathetic vibration]].
# {{label|en|chemistry}} The property of a [[compound]] that can be visualized as having two [[structure]]s differing only in the distribution of [[electron]]s; [[mesomerism]].
# {{label|en|astronomy}} A influence of the [[gravitational]] [[force]]s of one [[orbit]]ing object on the orbit of another, causing [[periodic]] [[perturbation]]s.
# {{label|en|electronics}} The condition where the [[inductive]] and [[capacitive]] [[reactance]]s have equal [[magnitude]]. 

